  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of constraints that shape the layout of a wind farm and explain why some turbines are kept while others are removed. Black turbine icons represent commissioned turbines arranged in a loose line, while gray turbines marked with red Xs indicate dismantled units. Around the active turbines, small dotted circles show a close spacing limit, and a larger dashed envelope indicates a broader distance rule tied to turbine height. An even larger irregular dashed zone marks an area where cumulative noise becomes problematic, especially near nearby settlements shown as clusters of small gray houses.\n\nThe overall message is that turbine placement is not determined by space alone. Several visible factors—noise, aesthetics, political bargaining, and available space—push certain turbines out of the final plan. Dismantled turbines tend to sit near houses or outside the preferred arrangement, suggesting they conflicted with setback or nuisance limits. The large pale-green outer boundary shows a much wider area influenced by the wind farm relative to turbine height. Overall, the diagram illustrates how technical spacing rules and social or environmental concerns combine to reduce the number of turbines that can actually be commissioned.",
